## Concerns tangled together

1. **Aggregation** — summing the valid items (the loop)
2. **Per-item business rules** — validity check + the threshold branching
3. **Side effects** — `log_warning`/`log_error`, `retry`, `skip`
4. **Persistence** — `save_state(total)`

## Concrete extractions I'd make

| Extraction | Pulls out | Why |
|---|---|---|
| `int sum_valid_items(const Input&)` | the loop (L3–10) | `process` becomes a thin orchestrator; aggregation is testable in isolation |
| `int process_item(const Item&)` | per-item logic (L4–9) | one item's rules in one place; the loop body shrinks to `total += process_item(item)` |
| `void apply_value_rules(const Item&, int&)` | the `if/else if/else` chain (L6–8) | the threshold policy (warn+retry / error+skip / +2 bonus) is the real business rule — isolate it |
| `void log_and_retry(const Item&)` / `void log_and_skip(const Item&)` | the log+action pairs (L6, L7) | optional; keeps side effects named and swappable |
| `void persist_total(int)` | `save_state` (L11) | optional; wraps the I/O boundary |

## Target shape

```cpp
int process(const Input& in) {
  int total = sum_valid_items(in);
  persist_total(total);
  return total;
}

int sum_valid_items(const Input& in) {
  int total = 0;
  for (auto& item : in.items)
    total += process_item(item);
  return total;
}

int process_item(const Item& item) {
  if (!item.valid) return 0;
  int contribution = item.value;
  apply_value_rules(item, contribution);
  return contribution;
}

void apply_value_rules(const Item& item, int& contribution) {
  if (item.value > 100)      { log_warning(item.id); retry(item); }
  else if (item.value < 0)   { log_error(item.id);   skip(item);  }
  else                       { contribution += 2; }
}

void persist_total(int total) { save_state(total); }
```

Note the semantics are preserved exactly: a valid item contributes `item.value`, plus `+2` only in the `0 ≤ value ≤ 100` band — the `>100` and `<0` branches add nothing extra, only side effects.

